[comment {-*- tcl -*- doctools manpage}]
[vset VERSION 0.1]
[manpage_begin map::mark n [vset VERSION]]
[category {Map Display and Supporting Utilities}][keywords map {location marking} {mark location}]
[moddesc   {Map display support}]
[titledesc {Map Action Engine - Mark A Point}]
[require Tcl 8.6-]
[require debug]
[require debug::caller]
[require snit]
[require map::mark [opt [vset VERSION]]]
[description]

This package provides a [package map::display] attachment extending the map's behaviour.
This attachment enables the user to mark locations, i.e. [term geo/point]s, by double-clicking on
the map. The marked locations are reported via a callback.

[section API]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::map::mark] [arg objectName] [arg map] [opt [arg options]...]]

Creates a new mark engine named [arg objectName], attaches it to the [arg map], and returns the
fully qualified name of its command. The [arg objectName] can be [const %AUTO%] to generate a
unique name. The engine is active after creation, see method [method enable].

[para] The [arg map] is a [package map::display] widget, or any other command providing the methods
[method canvas], returning the canvas widget showing the map, and [method at], returning the
geographical location under the mouse pointer.

[para] When the [option -on-event] occurs on the map, the location under the mouse pointer is
reported through the [option -command]. The options are:

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -command [arg cmdprefix]]
The command prefix called when a location is marked. It is called with one argument appended, the
marked location, a list of latitude and longitude in degrees (see [package map::slippy]). The
result of the callback is ignored. If empty (the default), marks are ignored.

[opt_def -on-event [arg event]]
The event marking a location, an event pattern as accepted by [cmd bind], without the enclosing
angle brackets. Defaults to [const Double-Button-1], i.e. a double-click with mouse button 1. This
option is read-only, it can only be set at creation.
[list_end]


[call [arg objectName] [method enable]]

Activates the engine, i.e. binds the [option -on-event] on the canvas of the map, replacing any
binding of the same event the canvas may have had. The call is ignored if the engine is already
active. The result is the empty string.

[call [arg objectName] [method disable]]

Deactivates the engine, i.e. removes the binding of the [option -on-event] from the map canvas.
The call is ignored if the engine is already inactive. The result is the empty string.

[call [arg objectName] [method active]]

Returns a boolean value, true if the engine is active, and false otherwise.

[call [arg objectName] [method configure] [opt [arg option]] [opt [arg value]]...]
[call [arg objectName] [method cget] [arg option]]

The standard methods to change and query the options.

[call [arg objectName] [method destroy]]

Destroys the engine, after deactivating it. The map is not destroyed.

[list_end]

[section EXAMPLE]

[example_begin]
package require Tk
package require map::display
package require map::provider::osm
package require map::mark

set cache [lb]file join $env(HOME) .cache map-tiles[rb]
file mkdir $cache
map provider osm Tiles $cache

map display .map -provider Tiles -initial-geo {49.2827 -123.1207} -initial-zoom 12
pack .map -fill both -expand 1

proc marked {geo} {
    lassign $geo lat lon
    puts "Marked $lat, $lon"
}

map mark Marker .map -command marked
[example_end]

[see_also map::display map::provider::osm]

